NAME
       mount - attach a file system to the directory tree

SYNOPSIS
       mount
       mount [-t TYPE] [-o OPTIONS] DEVICE DIRECTORY
       mount [-o OPTIONS] DEVICE|DIRECTORY
       mount -a [-v]

DESCRIPTION
       mount makes the file system on a device (a partition, logical volume,
       DVD, NFS export...) appear at a directory, the mount point. The
       directory must exist. Whatever was in it before is hidden until the
       file system is unmounted with umount.

       With no arguments, mount lists everything that is mounted (findmnt and
       df -h give tidier lists).

       With only a device or only a directory, mount looks for the rest in
       /etc/fstab. "mount -a" mounts every fstab entry that is not mounted
       yet, except those with noauto. Run it after editing /etc/fstab: it is
       the best check that the next boot will work, because a broken line
       can stop the system at boot.

       After editing /etc/fstab, systemd keeps its old copy of the file until
       "systemctl daemon-reload"; mount prints a hint about this.

       The device can be given as a path (/dev/vdb1, /dev/vgdata/data), as
       UUID=... or LABEL=..., and for NFS as SERVER:/PATH. mount usually
       finds the file system type itself; -t sets it.

       Mounts made with the mount command last until reboot. To make them
       permanent, add a line to /etc/fstab:

           DEVICE  MOUNTPOINT  TYPE  OPTIONS  DUMP  FSCK

       for example "UUID=3f2a... /data xfs defaults 0 0".

OPTIONS
       -t, --types TYPE
              The file system type: xfs, ext4, vfat, nfs, iso9660, swap...

       -o, --options LIST
              Mount options, separated by commas (see below).

       -a, --all
              Mount every file system in /etc/fstab (except noauto).

       -r, --read-only
              Mount read-only (same as -o ro).

       -w, --rw
              Mount read-write (the default).

       -L, --label LABEL
              Mount the file system with this label.

       -U, --uuid UUID
              Mount the file system with this UUID.

       -B, --bind
              Make a directory also appear at a second place.

       -v, --verbose
              Say what is done.

MOUNT OPTIONS
       defaults
              The usual set: rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async.

       ro, rw Read-only or read-write.

       remount
              Change the options of a mounted file system without unmounting
              it, such as "mount -o remount,rw /sysroot" in the rd.break
              shell.

       noexec, nosuid, nodev
              Do not run programs, ignore set-user-ID bits, ignore device
              files on this file system.

       noauto Do not mount with mount -a or at boot.

       nofail Do not stop the boot if the device is missing.

       _netdev
              The file system needs the network (NFS, iSCSI): mount it after
              the network is up.

       context=CONTEXT
              Give every file one SELinux context, for file systems without
              labels (vfat, NFS).

       uid=, gid=, umask=
              Owner, group and permission mask for every file on vfat.

EXAMPLES
       Mount the RHEL DVD to use as a package source:

           mount /dev/sr0 /mnt

       Mount an XFS partition by hand, then check it:

           mkdir /data
           mount /dev/vdb1 /data
           findmnt /data

       After adding a line to /etc/fstab:

           systemctl daemon-reload
           mount -a

       Mount an NFS export from utility.lab.example.com:

           mount -t nfs utility.lab.example.com:/exports/shared /mnt/shared

       Make the real root file system writable in the rd.break shell:

           mount -o remount,rw /sysroot

FILES
       /etc/fstab
              The file systems to mount at boot.

       /proc/mounts
              What is mounted now.

SEE ALSO
       umount(8), findmnt(8), fstab(5), blkid(8), lsblk(8), mount.nfs(8),
       swapon(8), systemd.mount(5)
